---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: "What is the defining trait of a message in the traditional queue paradigm?",
    options: [
      "It is a self-contained record of a fact that can be replayed by any number of independent readers",
      "It is a command with direct intent, addressed to one consumer that is expected to act on it and confirm",
      "It is a broadcast notification that any number of unrelated systems can react to independently",
      "It is an immutable log entry that a producer appends without knowing who will read it"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "A message is addressed to a specific consumer and expects that consumer to execute an action, GenerateInvoice or SendEmail, and confirm it was done."
  },
  {
    question: "What is the defining trait of an event?",
    options: [
      "It is a command telling one specific downstream system exactly what action to perform next",
      "It is an immutable, self-contained record of a fact that already happened, published without knowing who will read it",
      "It is a request that blocks the producer until the consumer sends back a confirmation",
      "It is a temporary record that gets deleted from the broker the moment one consumer reads it"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "An event describes a fact, like PaymentCompleted. The producer publishes it without caring who consumes it or how many consumers there are."
  },
  {
    question: "A producer publishes PaymentCompleted. Billing, analytics, and fraud detection all react to it independently. What does this demonstrate?",
    options: [
      "Broadcast, decoupled architecture: the producer does not need to know who consumes the event",
      "Point-to-point coupling, since the producer must explicitly address each of the three consumers",
      "That PaymentCompleted is actually a command being routed to three different queues",
      "That the event gets deleted once the first of the three consumers reads it"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "Multiple independent systems can react to the same fact without the producer being aware of any of them. That is the broadcast property events give you."
  },
  {
    question: "You see an event named GenerateInvoice in a system. What does this pill say that name reveals?",
    options: [
      "That the event was published to a queue instead of a log-based topic",
      "That it is likely a command disguised as an event, hinting at hidden point-to-point coupling",
      "That the system is using a stream-to-table join internally",
      "That the payload is probably too large to fit in a single Kafka message"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "A verb aimed at a specific system, like GenerateInvoice, reads like a command. If your 'events' are full of these, you have built a distributed RPC system, not a streaming pipeline."
  },
  {
    question: "What breaks first when you add a new consumer to a system built entirely on messages instead of events?",
    options: [
      "The broker automatically discovers the new consumer and requires no changes at all",
      "The sender usually needs to change, because it has to know who to address the new command to",
      "The original consumer stops receiving anything until the new one is removed",
      "Every existing message in the queue gets duplicated for the new consumer"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Messages create point-to-point dependencies. Adding a consumer to an event-based system just means pointing a new reader at the existing topic, with no change upstream."
  },
  {
    question: "Which naming test does Pill 1 suggest for telling events and messages apart?",
    options: [
      "Check if the name reads like a fact that already happened versus a verb aimed at a specific system",
      "Check whether the payload is serialized as JSON or as Avro",
      "Check how many partitions the underlying topic has been configured with",
      "Check whether the message was published synchronously or asynchronously"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "OrderShipped reads like a fact. ChargeCard reads like a command. That distinction, not the transport, is what separates an event from a message."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Quiz: Events vs Messages | Streaming Pill 1"
  description="Test your understanding of why an event is a fact and a message is a command, and why the distinction changes system coupling."
>
  <section class="pill-quiz-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-1-events-vs-messages" class="back-link">← Back to Pill 1</a>

      <div class="pill-header">
        <div class="badge badge-series" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pill 1 · Events vs Messages</div>
        <h1>Quiz: Fact or Command?</h1>
        <p class="pill-lead">
          These questions test whether you can tell an event from a message by what it does to
          coupling, not just by which broker carries it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Quiz
        title="Pill 1: Events Are Not Messages"
        questions={questions}
      />

      <div class="pill-footer glass-card">
        <h3>How did it go?</h3>
        <p>
          If you scored below 70%, re-read <strong>Pill 1</strong> and look at the names of
          events in a system you know. Do any of them read like commands aimed at one consumer?
        </p>
        <div class="pill-footer-links">
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-1-events-vs-messages" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Re-read Pill 1</a>
          <a href="/pills/streaming-quiz-2" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Next: Pill 2 Quiz — Two Clocks</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pill-quiz-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .badge-series {
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .pill-header {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .pill-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.5vw, 2.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .pill-lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .pill-footer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .pill-footer h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-main);
  }

  .pill-footer p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pill-footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
  }
</style>
